  "image_name": "15c4e0e21d9a310b72ab5fa5de21f43f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two ways of thinking about land carbon storage: a “silo” view and a broader “haystack” view. The silo side represents a constrained estimate, showing carbon uptake as limited mainly to refilling a relatively small storage space, around 100 GtCO2. The haystack side shows a much larger and still expanding potential, reaching on the order of 1,000 GtCO2, implying that land carbon storage could increase beyond what has historically been observed.\n\nThe comparison is organized around three drivers. In biogeography, the silo view assumes ecosystems have limited ability to shift toward higher-carbon vegetation, whereas the haystack view emphasizes substantial gains through vegetation change, such as afforestation of grasslands. In biogeochemistry, the limited view suggests CO2, nitrogen, and phosphorus inputs eventually slow or saturate, while the broader view suggests these effects can continue or grow. Under management choices, the silo perspective focuses on regrowth and reforestation but also highlights losses from disturbance and mortality as temperatures rise. The haystack perspective instead shows that large-scale plantations, afforestation, and improved management or fire suppression could maintain or increase carbon stocks.",
